SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 308 | Tuesday, March 10, 2026

Good morning.

1) Port-fee hearing: senators from both parties like the idea, hate the pass-through
The proposed fee on foreign-built vessels calling at US ports drew support in principle and a lot of questions about who pays. Carriers testified they would add a per-container surcharge; a retailer's supply chain chief testified it would add $200 per FEU to landed cost. Nobody mentioned that US shipyards cannot build a container ship of any relevant size before 2032.

2) Polyethylene up another 5 cents
The Gulf Coast cracker outage plus a scheduled turnaround at a second plant have taken about 9% of North American ethylene capacity offline. Converters are on allocation. If you buy stretch film, pallets wrap, or anything in a poly bag, expect a surcharge letter this month.

3) Cascade Supply Chain Services loses its largest account
The 4PL confirmed that a national grocery chain is taking its transportation management in-house after eleven years. The account was about a quarter of Cascade's revenue. Two things worth noticing: the grocer built the replacement on a mid-market TMS in nine months, and it hired Cascade's account team to run it.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 190. Days of forward coverage of rare-earth magnets held by a large US appliance maker, per its annual filing, up from 45 days two years ago. Inventory is the new hedge.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

